diff --git a/docs/ANDROID-SIGNING.md b/docs/ANDROID-SIGNING.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1d95f0d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ANDROID-SIGNING.md
@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+# Android Release Signing
+
+## Status of the old key
+`app/android/ednova-release.keystore` (password in the former `signAndroidRelease.ts`) was committed to git.
+**Treat it as compromised.** It must never be used for a Play Store release. It remains in git history;
+since the key is retired, history rewriting is optional (do it if the repo is or becomes public).
+
+## Strategy: Play App Signing (required for new apps on Google Play)
+Google holds the *app signing key*. You keep only an *upload key*, which is replaceable if lost or leaked.
+
+1. Generate a NEW upload key, outside the repo:
+   ```bash
+   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore ~/secure/ednova-upload.keystore -alias ednova-upload \
+     -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000
+   ```
+   Use a strong, unique password. Store keystore + password in a password manager and an encrypted backup.
+2. Local builds: copy `app/android/keystore.properties.example` to `keystore.properties` (gitignored) and fill it in.
+3. CI builds: set `EDNOVA_KEYSTORE_PATH`, `EDNOVA_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `EDNOVA_KEY_ALIAS`, `EDNOVA_KEY_PASSWORD`
+   from encrypted secrets (decode the keystore from a base64 secret to a temp file).
+4. Build: `cd app && npx tsx scripts/buildAndroidRelease.ts` -> upload the `.aab` to Play Console.
+5. In Play Console, enrol in Play App Signing on first upload.
+
+## Notes
+- Debug builds (`./gradlew assembleDebug`) need no credentials and are unaffected.
+- If no credentials are present, `assembleRelease` produces an *unsigned* APK instead of failing silently with a wrong key.
+- Releases are signed by Gradle (v1/v2/v3 + zipalign). The previous `jarsigner` approach produced v1-only signatures,
+  which Android 11+ rejects for modern targetSdk.
-- 
